# Reference checklists

<a id="security"></a>

## Security and authentication

Mark each item PASS / FAIL / N/A / CAN'T TELL, with evidence. 🔴 blocks launch · 🟡 before scale · 🟢 recommended.

### Authentication and sessions

- [ ] 🔴 **Proven auth provider.** Supabase Auth, Clerk, Auth0, Firebase Auth, NextAuth/Auth.js, Lucia or similar. No hand-rolled password or JWT logic.
- [ ] 🔴 **Sessions end.** Sessions expire, logout invalidates them on the server, and a password change ends other sessions.
- [ ] 🔴 **Cookie flags.** Auth cookies are `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict`. No long-lived tokens in `localStorage`.
- [ ] 🔴 **JWTs verified properly.** Signature checked, `alg: none` rejected, expiry enforced, and the secret is strong and kept on the server.
- [ ] 🔴 **Password reset and magic links.** Tokens are single-use, expire in 15–60 minutes, and are stored hashed. The response is the same whether or not the email exists.
- [ ] 🔴 **Email verification** before the account can do anything sensitive (if it matters for this product).
- [ ] 🔴 **Brute-force protection.** Rate limits on login, OTP, signup, password reset and verification resends, by IP and by account.
- [ ] 🟡 **Account lockout or CAPTCHA** after repeated failures, without letting attackers permanently lock out real users.
- [ ] 🟡 **Token expiry and refresh rotation.** Short-lived access tokens, rotating refresh tokens, and a way to revoke them.
- [ ] 🟡 **2FA**, at least for admin accounts.
- [ ] 🟡 **OAuth done right.** `state` parameter used, redirect URIs exact-matched (no wildcards), and accounts aren't auto-linked by unverified email.
- [ ] 🟢 **Passkeys** if the provider supports them.
- [ ] 🟢 **SSO (SAML/OIDC)** if selling to companies.

### Access control

- [ ] 🔴 **Authorization on every request.** Checked on the server for every route, server action, RPC and edge function, not just in the UI or middleware.
- [ ] 🔴 **No IDOR.** Changing an ID in a URL or body never exposes or changes another user's data. Prefer UUIDs, but still check ownership.
- [ ] 🔴 **Roles in one place.** Defined centrally and enforced on the server. Users can't change their own role.
- [ ] 🔴 **Row-Level Security / security rules.** On for every table, collection and storage bucket, with policies for select, insert, update and delete. `WITH CHECK` on insert and update so users can't write rows for others.
- [ ] 🔴 **No mass assignment.** Writes allow-list fields.
- [ ] 🔴 **Least privilege.** DB users, service accounts, API keys, CI tokens and team members get only what they need. The service-role or admin SDK is never used where the user's own session would do.
- [ ] 🔴 **Multi-tenant isolation** (if there are teams or orgs). Every query is scoped by tenant, and invites can't be replayed or used to join other orgs.
- [ ] 🟡 **Audit log** for admin actions, permission changes, payments, exports and deletions.

### Secrets and keys

- [ ] 🔴 **No secrets in the repo, git history, client bundle or public env vars.** If one has ever leaked, rotate it. Deleting the line is not enough.
- [ ] 🔴 **Separate keys per environment.** Test keys in dev and staging, live keys only in production.
- [ ] 🔴 **The app's own API keys** (if you issue them) are stored hashed, scoped and revocable.
- [ ] 🟡 **Rotation plan.** A documented way to rotate every secret quickly.
- [ ] 🟡 **Secret scanning on.** GitHub secret scanning and push protection, or gitleaks/trufflehog in CI.

### Injection and input

- [ ] 🔴 **Input validation on the server** of type, length, format and range, using a schema (Zod, Valibot, Joi, Pydantic).
- [ ] 🔴 **SQL injection.** Parameterized queries or an ORM only. No string-concatenated SQL, including in `.rpc()`, raw queries and `sql.unsafe`.
- [ ] 🔴 **NoSQL injection.** No user objects passed straight into Mongo or Firestore queries (`{ $gt: "" }`).
- [ ] 🔴 **XSS.** No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `v-html`, `innerHTML` or markdown-to-HTML on user content without sanitizing (DOMPurify). URLs from users are checked against `javascript:`.
- [ ] 🔴 **Command injection.** No `exec`, `spawn` with a shell, `eval`, `new Function` or `child_process` with user input.
- [ ] 🔴 **File uploads.** Type is checked by content (not just extension), size is limited, files get random names, they're stored in a private bucket with signed URLs, and SVG/HTML aren't served inline from your domain.
- [ ] 🟡 **Path traversal.** File paths are never built from user input (`../../`).
- [ ] 🟡 **SSRF.** Server-side fetches of URLs users supply block private IP ranges and cloud metadata (`169.254.169.254`).
- [ ] 🟡 **Open redirects.** `?next=`, `?redirect=` and `?returnTo=` only allow relative paths or an allow-list.
- [ ] 🟡 **CSRF.** SameSite cookies, plus CSRF tokens or origin checks for state-changing requests that use cookie auth.
- [ ] 🟡 **CORS.** No `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` together with credentials. Allowed origins are an explicit list.
- [ ] 🟡 **Deserialization.** No `pickle`, `yaml.load` (unsafe) or `unserialize` on untrusted input.
- [ ] 🟡 **Regex DoS.** No catastrophic regex on user input.

### Transport, headers and crypto

- [ ] 🔴 **HTTPS everywhere**, with HTTP redirected to HTTPS and HSTS on.
- [ ] 🔴 **Passwords hashed** with argon2id or bcrypt (salted automatically). Never encrypted, plain text, MD5 or SHA-*.
- [ ] 🟡 **Hashing vs encryption.** Hashing for things you only verify (passwords, tokens); encryption for things you need back (keys for third-party accounts and similar).
- [ ] 🔴 **Encryption at rest** for the DB, file storage and backups (check the provider's setting; most managed DBs and storage encrypt by default, so confirm it). 🔴 for apps with children's, health, financial or ID data; 🟡 otherwise.
- [ ] 🔴 **Field-level encryption for very sensitive data** (government IDs, health records, bank details, stored OAuth or API tokens of users), with keys kept outside the DB. N/A if the app stores none.
- [ ] 🔴 **Encryption in transit** everywhere: HTTPS for the app and API, TLS to the DB, storage and between services, and no `http://` calls to third parties.
- [ ] 🟡 **Security headers:** `Content-Security-Policy` (start report-only), `frame-ancestors` or `X-Frame-Options` (clickjacking), `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy`, `Permissions-Policy`. Check with securityheaders.com.
- [ ] 🟡 **Cryptographic randomness** for tokens and IDs (`crypto.randomUUID` or `crypto.getRandomValues`, never `Math.random`).

### Dependencies and supply chain

- [ ] 🔴 **`npm audit` / `pnpm audit` / `pip-audit` / `bundle audit`** run, with no unresolved critical or high findings that are reachable.
- [ ] 🔴 **Every package is real and reputable:** check downloads, maintainers and last release. Watch for typosquats.
- [ ] 🟡 **Lockfile committed** and versions pinned. Dependabot or Renovate enabled.
- [ ] 🟡 **No unneeded packages.** Remove what isn't used.
- [ ] 🟢 **Pentest.** At least an OWASP ZAP baseline scan against staging. A professional pentest before enterprise deals or sensitive data.

### Rate limiting and abuse

- [ ] 🔴 **Rate limits** on auth, password reset, OTP and SMS (to prevent SMS-pumping fraud), email sending, LLM calls, file uploads, search, exports and any expensive endpoint.
- [ ] 🟡 **Bot protection** on signup and public forms (Turnstile, hCaptcha, honeypot).

### Data leakage

Check how data could reach someone who shouldn't see it, even when access control looks right.

- [ ] 🔴 **API responses return only what the screen needs:** no password hashes, tokens, internal flags, other users' emails or phone numbers, or whole database rows (`select *` sent to the client). Check list endpoints, search, public profiles, exports and server-rendered page data.
- [ ] 🔴 **No account enumeration:** signup, login, password reset and invite flows don't reveal whether an email or phone number is registered (same message and similar timing), or, where the product must say so, they're rate-limited.
- [ ] 🔴 **Private data isn't cached for others:** responses with personal data send `Cache-Control: private, no-store`. CDN, edge and framework caches never serve one user's page or API response to another.
- [ ] 🔴 **No secrets or personal data in URLs:** tokens, emails and IDs aren't in query strings (they end up in logs, browser history, analytics and `Referer` headers). Reset and magic links are single-use, and `Referrer-Policy` is set.
- [ ] 🔴 **Share and file links aren't guessable:** long random IDs or signed, expiring URLs for shared reports, invoices, uploads and exports. No sequential IDs.
- [ ] 🟡 **Third parties get only what they need:** analytics, error tracking, session replay, chat widgets and AI providers don't receive passwords, form contents, health data or children's data. Session replay masks inputs. Error tracking scrubs personal data.
- [ ] 🟡 **Client bundles and source don't expose internals:** no admin routes, internal API keys, feature flags for unreleased paid features, or other customers' data in JavaScript bundles, HTML comments or `__NEXT_DATA__` and similar.
- [ ] 🟡 **Exports and downloads** (CSV, PDF, backups) are access-checked and logged, include only the requester's data, and are protected against CSV formula injection (`=`, `+`, `-`, `@` at the start of cells).
- [ ] 🟡 **No sensitive data in emails and notifications:** they don't include sensitive data in subject lines or lock-screen previews, and go only to verified addresses.
- [ ] 🟡 **Deleted data is really gone:** deleted accounts, files and messages are removed from search indexes, caches, CDN, storage buckets and third parties within the stated time.

<a id="reliability"></a>

## Data, reliability and testing

Code-level reliability: APIs, data, background work, performance and tests. Deployment, monitoring, backups and operations are in the **devops** section.

Mark each item PASS / FAIL / N/A / CAN'T TELL, with evidence. 🔴 blocks launch · 🟡 before scale · 🟢 recommended.

### APIs and error handling

- [ ] 🔴 **Correct HTTP status codes:** 200/201/204, 400/401/403/404/409/422/429, 500/503. No "200 with an error body".
- [ ] 🔴 **Errors handled.** Users see a friendly message, logs get the details, and stack traces and SQL errors never reach the client.
- [ ] 🔴 **Timeouts** on every outbound HTTP, DB and LLM call, and on serverless functions.
- [ ] 🔴 **Idempotency.** Payment, order and signup endpoints and webhook handlers can safely run twice (idempotency keys, unique constraints, upserts).
- [ ] 🟡 **Retries with exponential backoff and jitter**, with a maximum. Retry only safe or idempotent operations.
- [ ] 🟡 **Webhooks.** Signature verified, 200 returned fast, work done in a background job, duplicate and out-of-order events handled, and a replay path exists.
- [ ] 🟡 **API versioning** (`/v1/` or a header) if mobile apps or third parties call the API.
- [ ] 🟡 **API documentation** (OpenAPI or a README) for any public or partner API.
- [ ] 🟢 **Circuit breakers or fallbacks** for flaky dependencies.

### Database and data

- [ ] 🔴 **Migrations.** Every schema change is a versioned migration in git. No manual edits in the production dashboard.
- [ ] 🔴 **Indexes** on the columns the code filters, joins and sorts on (foreign keys, `user_id`, `created_at`, slugs). Check them against the actual queries.
- [ ] 🔴 **Constraints in the DB:** `NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, foreign keys and check constraints, so bad data can't get in even if app code has a bug.
- [ ] 🟡 **Normalized schema**, with no duplicated data that can drift apart. Denormalize only on purpose.
- [ ] 🟡 **Soft deletes** (`deleted_at`) where users may want data back, and **hard deletes** for privacy erasure requests.
- [ ] 🟡 **Connection pooling.** Required on serverless (Supabase pooler, PgBouncer, Neon pooling, Prisma Accelerate).
- [ ] 🟡 **Transactions** around multi-step writes.
- [ ] 🟡 **Optimistic locking** (version column or `updated_at` check) where two users can edit the same thing.
- [ ] 🟡 **UTC timestamps** (`timestamptz`), converted only for display.
- [ ] 🟡 **UTF-8 end to end** (DB, APIs, CSV exports with a BOM for Excel).
- [ ] 🟡 **Pagination** on every list, with limits on page size.
- [ ] 🟢 **Read replicas** when read load outgrows one DB.

### Background work

- [ ] 🟡 **Slow work runs in the background:** emails, AI calls, image or video processing, reports and imports run in a queue, not in the request.
- [ ] 🟡 **Queue with retries and a dead-letter queue** (BullMQ, SQS, Inngest, Trigger.dev, QStash, Cloud Tasks).
- [ ] 🟡 **Cron jobs** can't overlap and are idempotent. (Monitoring them is in the **devops** section.)
- [ ] 🟡 **Cleanup jobs run on a schedule,** not as a side effect of user actions. For example, deleting expired uploads, old logs and sessions shouldn't depend on a user coming back or an admin opening a page.

### Performance and scaling

- [ ] 🟡 **Caching** of expensive reads, with an explicit expiry.
- [ ] 🟡 **Cache invalidation** decided for each cache. Prices, permissions and plan limits are never served stale.
- [ ] 🟡 **CDN** for static assets, and images resized and served in modern formats.
- [ ] 🟡 **Stateless services.** No sessions, uploads or job state in server memory or on local disk.
- [ ] 🟡 **Async correctness.** No missing `await`, no unhandled promise rejections, and independent calls run in parallel (`Promise.all`).
- [ ] 🟡 **Memory leaks.** Listeners, intervals, subscriptions and DB connections are cleaned up.
- [ ] 🟡 **Concurrency.** Shared counters and limits are updated atomically.
- [ ] 🟢 **Load balancing**, reverse proxy (Nginx, Caddy, Cloudflare) and API gateway when the architecture needs them.
- [ ] 🟢 **Horizontal vs vertical scaling.** Know which your setup supports and where it stops.
- [ ] 🟢 **Profiling** before optimizing.

### Testing and process

- [ ] 🔴 **Tests for the money and safety paths,** including a **replay test** for each payment and webhook route: signup, login, password reset, payment, permissions ("user A can't touch user B"), and data deletion.
- [ ] 🟡 **Test pyramid.** Unit tests for logic, integration tests for API plus DB, and E2E (Playwright, Cypress) for the main journeys.
- [ ] 🟡 **Code review.** A human reviews changes to auth, payments, data access, migrations and infrastructure.
- [ ] 🟡 **Branching strategy.** Short-lived branches; main is always deployable.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tech debt written down**, with the shortcuts listed.
- [ ] 🟡 **Project rules file** (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.cursor/rules`) recording architecture, conventions and "never do" rules, so future AI sessions stay consistent.
- [ ] 🟢 **Semantic versioning** for APIs, packages and app releases.
- [ ] 🟢 **Docs.** A README covering setup, environment variables and deployment, so someone else could run it.

### Load and stress

**Never load-test or stress-test production, or anything you don't own.** Run these on staging or a local copy, only with the user's approval, and keep paid APIs (AI, SMS, email, payments) in test or mock mode so a test can't create a bill. If there's no safe environment, review the code for the risks below and list the test as "Not checked", with the command for the user.

- [ ] 🔴 **Expensive endpoints are protected before any load arrives:** rate limits, request size limits, pagination caps, timeouts and per-user quotas on AI, uploads, search, exports and sign-ups (see the **security** section and the **ai-features** section).
- [ ] 🟡 **Load test of the main journeys** before launch or a campaign, with a tool such as k6, Artillery, autocannon or Locust: expected peak users × 2, measuring p95 response time and error rate. Record the result and the date.
- [ ] 🟡 **Spike test:** a sudden jump (e.g. 10× normal for 1–2 minutes). The app slows down or queues work, but doesn't crash, corrupt data or double-charge.
- [ ] 🟡 **Realistic data volume:** pages and queries tested with production-sized data (e.g. 100k rows, users with 1,000 items), not just a few test records. Watch for slow queries and memory use.
- [ ] 🟡 **Concurrency test of money and limits:** many parallel requests to payment, credit, coupon and quota endpoints prove there are no double spends or limit bypasses.
- [ ] 🟡 **Graceful overload:** when limits are hit, users get a clear "busy, try again" message (HTTP 429/503 with `Retry-After`), and background queues absorb bursts.
- [ ] 🟡 **Platform limits known:** the serverless timeout, memory, DB connection limit, storage and AI provider rate limits, and what happens when each is hit.
- [ ] 🟢 **Soak test:** a few hours at normal load shows no memory leaks or growing response times.

<a id="devops"></a>

## DevOps and operations

Load this file for every Full and Enterprise check. It covers how the app is built, deployed, watched, backed up and recovered, and who can operate it. Many items are settings or processes rather than code: check config files (`.github/workflows`, `wrangler.*`, `vercel.json`, `netlify.toml`, `fly.toml`, `Dockerfile`, `package.json` scripts, IaC), run safe read-only commands, and ask the user for anything that lives in a dashboard. Record the evidence or the question for each item.

### CI and code flow
- [ ] 🔴 **CI runs on every push and PR:** install, lint, type-check, tests and build (e.g. a GitHub Actions workflow). A local "release check" script doesn't count unless deploys can't skip it.
- [ ] 🔴 **The main branch is protected:** PRs and passing checks required, no force-pushes. → ask the user, or check the repo settings with `gh api` if available.
- [ ] 🟡 **Dependency updates are automated** (Dependabot or Renovate), with security alerts on.
- [ ] 🟡 **Secret scanning and push protection** are on for the repo.
- [ ] 🟡 **Reproducible builds:** lockfile committed, runtime version pinned (`.nvmrc`, `engines`, `.tool-versions`, `runtime.txt`).

### Environments and deploys
- [ ] 🔴 **Dev, staging and production are separate,** with separate DBs, storage buckets, keys, webhooks and OAuth apps. Preview deploys never use production data or live keys.
- [ ] 🔴 **Deploys are automated and traceable:** every production deploy maps to a commit, and the tests run before it.
- [ ] 🔴 **Rollback in minutes:** a known way to go back to the previous version (platform rollback, redeploy a tag), plus how to handle DB migrations when rolling back. → ask if not documented.
- [ ] 🟡 **Migrations run as part of the deploy,** are backwards-compatible where possible, and are tested on staging first.
- [ ] 🟡 **Feature flags or a kill switch** for risky or costly features (AI, payments, sign-ups).
- [ ] 🟡 **Platform default domains** (`*.workers.dev`, `*.vercel.app`, `*.netlify.app`, `*.pages.dev`, `*.fly.dev`) are disabled or redirect to the real domain.
- [ ] 🟢 **Infrastructure as Code** (Terraform, Pulumi, SST, wrangler config in git), so the setup can be rebuilt.
- [ ] 🟢 **Graceful shutdown:** in-flight requests and jobs finish before the process stops (for long-running servers).

### Monitoring and alerting
- [ ] 🔴 **Error tracking with alerts:** production errors (frontend and backend) go to a tool that **notifies a human** (Sentry, PostHog, Bugsnag, Rollbar, Cloudflare/Vercel alerts). An admin page that nobody watches doesn't count.
- [ ] 🔴 **Outside uptime monitoring** of the main site and API (BetterStack, UptimeRobot, Checkly), with alerts to email, phone or Slack.
- [ ] 🔴 **Scheduled jobs are monitored:** a cron or heartbeat monitor alerts if a job fails **or stops running** (e.g. Healthchecks.io, Cronitor, Sentry Crons).
- [ ] 🟡 **A health check endpoint** (`/health`) that checks the DB and critical dependencies, used by the uptime monitor.
- [ ] 🟡 **Third-party outages are handled:** calls to AI, payment, auth and email providers have timeouts, limited retries, and a friendly fallback message. Check each provider the app calls.
- [ ] 🟡 **Structured logs** with request ID, user ID and route, kept for a defined period, searchable when something breaks.
- [ ] 🟡 **Alerts are actionable:** few and meaningful (error spikes, downtime, failed jobs, payment webhook failures), each with a clear owner.
- [ ] 🟢 **Dashboards** for traffic, latency, error rate and resource use.
- [ ] 🟢 **Status page,** or another way to tell users about outages.

### Logs and data in operations
- [ ] 🔴 **No secrets or sensitive personal data in logs:** grep logging calls and error handlers for passwords, tokens, auth headers, card data, dates of birth, health data and full request bodies.
- [ ] 🟡 **Log retention matches the privacy policy,** and is enforced by a scheduled job or platform setting, not manually.
- [ ] 🟡 **Admin and support tools** that show user data have their own auth, 2FA and an access log.

### Backups and recovery
- [ ] 🔴 **Automatic, off-site backups** of the database (point-in-time recovery if available) and of important file storage.
- [ ] 🔴 **A restore has been tested,** with a date recorded and how long it took. → ask the user. Never tested = FAIL.
- [ ] 🔴 **Disaster recovery plan:** what happens if the DB is deleted, a key leaks, the cloud, GitHub or app-store account is locked, or the only developer is unavailable.
- [ ] 🟡 **Recovery targets known:** how much data could be lost (RPO) and how long recovery would take (RTO).

### Secrets and access
- [ ] 🔴 **Production secrets live in the platform's secret store,** not in config files or the repo.
- [ ] 🔴 **MFA on every critical account:** cloud, GitHub, domain registrar, DNS, payment provider, email provider, app stores, AI providers. → ask the user.
- [ ] 🟡 **Secret rotation:** a documented way to rotate each key quickly, and rotation after anyone with access leaves.
- [ ] 🟡 **Bus factor:** at least two trusted people can access and operate production. Accounts belong to the company, not one personal login.
- [ ] 🟡 **Least privilege for people and tokens:** CI tokens, deploy keys and team members get only the access they need.

### Costs, domains and certificates
- [ ] 🔴 **Billing alerts and spending caps** on cloud, database, storage, AI, SMS and email providers. → ask the user.
- [ ] 🟡 **Domain auto-renew is on,** with registrar lock and 2FA, and the expiry date is known.
- [ ] 🟡 **SSL/TLS certificates renew automatically,** and an expiry alert exists (many uptime tools include this).
- [ ] 🟡 **Email sending domain** has SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and transactional email (verification, password reset, receipts, alerts) actually arrives. Check that the app has an email provider at all.

### Incidents
- [ ] 🟡 **Incident runbook:** who gets alerted, first steps (roll back, disable feature, rotate key), how users are told, and the legal notification timelines (e.g. CERT-In 6 hours in India, GDPR 72 hours).
- [ ] 🟢 **Post-incident notes** are written, so the same failure doesn't repeat.

<a id="functional-qa"></a>

## Functional QA: does everything actually work?

AI-built apps often ship buttons that do nothing, links to pages that don't exist, and forms that look like they submit but don't. Check that every interactive element leads somewhere real.

**Method, best first:**
1. **Click-through (preferred):** run the app (`npm run dev`) or use staging, then use Playwright, a browser tool, or the vibe-check connector's `crawl_app`. Visit every route, click every button and link, and submit every form with valid and invalid data. On production, only follow links and open pages; never submit forms or create data.
2. **Static review (fallback):** trace every `onClick`, `onSubmit`, `<a href>`, `<Link to>`, `router.push` and form `action` to a handler or route that exists and does something.

Record each broken item with the page, the element (its text or selector), what happened, and what was expected. Include a screenshot if you can take one.

### Navigation and links
- [ ] 🔴 **No dead links:** every internal link and route resolves (no 404s). `href="#"` or `javascript:void(0)` appear only where a handler exists.
- [ ] 🔴 **No dead buttons:** every button has a handler that does something visible, or is clearly disabled with a reason.
- [ ] 🔴 **Main journeys work end to end:** signup → verify → onboarding → core action → result, plus login, logout and password reset.
- [ ] 🟡 **External links work** and open safely (`rel="noopener"` on `target="_blank"`).
- [ ] 🟡 **Back button and refresh** don't break state or re-submit forms.
- [ ] 🟡 **Deep links** to every page work when opened directly, logged in and logged out (with a sensible redirect to login).

### Forms
- [ ] 🔴 **Every form submits and saves**, and shows clear success feedback.
- [ ] 🔴 **Validation errors appear** next to the field, in words. The form keeps the user's input after an error.
- [ ] 🟡 **Double submit is prevented:** the button is disabled while a request is in flight.
- [ ] 🟡 **Long, empty, unicode and emoji input** doesn't break the layout or the save.
- [ ] 🟡 **File inputs** reject wrong types and sizes with a clear message.

### States
- [ ] 🔴 **Loading, empty and error states** exist for every screen that fetches data. No infinite spinners and no blank pages.
- [ ] 🟡 **Placeholder content is gone:** no lorem ipsum, "TODO", sample users, dummy prices, or `example.com` links.
- [ ] 🟡 **Permissions in the UI match the server:** users don't see buttons for actions they aren't allowed to do, and hidden buttons are still blocked on the server.
- [ ] 🟡 **Feature flags and half-built features** are hidden from users.

### Errors and assets
- [ ] 🔴 **No uncaught console errors** on the main pages.
- [ ] 🟡 **No failed network requests** (4xx/5xx) during normal use.
- [ ] 🟡 **No broken images, fonts or icons** (check for 404 assets).
- [ ] 🟡 **Emails and notifications** link to working pages (verification, reset, invites).

### Cross-device
- [ ] 🔴 **Main journeys work on a phone** (375px wide) and in Safari (iOS) and Chrome.
- [ ] 🟡 **Slow network** (throttle to "Fast 3G" or "Slow 4G"): no broken state, and timeouts show a message.

### Messages and microcopy
- [ ] 🔴 **Error messages are clear and helpful:** they say what went wrong and what to do next, in plain words ("Your card was declined. Try another card."). No raw codes, "undefined", "null", `[object Object]`, stack traces or "Something went wrong" with no way forward.
- [ ] 🔴 **Every important action confirms success:** saving, sending, paying, uploading, inviting and deleting all show a visible confirmation, and the result appears without a refresh.
- [ ] 🔴 **Destructive actions ask first:** deleting, cancelling a subscription, leaving a team or removing data shows a confirmation that names what will be lost. Undo is offered where it's cheap.
- [ ] 🟡 **Payment results are unambiguous:** success, failure, pending and cancelled each have a clear screen. Users are never left unsure whether they were charged.
- [ ] 🟡 **Messages are consistent:** same tone, terms and capitalisation across the app (e.g. "Delete" vs "Remove"), and in the users' language(s).
- [ ] 🟡 **No typos or grammar mistakes** in UI text, emails, error messages and legal pages. Run a spell-check over the copy.
- [ ] 🟡 **Empty and first-use states guide the user:** a short explanation and a clear next action, not a blank screen.
- [ ] 🟢 **Notifications and toasts** stay long enough to read, can be dismissed, and are announced to screen readers.

### Edge cases
- [ ] 🔴 **Money and numbers are correct:** prices, taxes, discounts and totals round correctly (integers in the smallest currency unit, no floating-point errors), with the right currency symbol and format. Zero, negative and very large amounts are handled.
- [ ] 🟡 **Unusual input works:** very long names and text, empty optional fields, emoji, non-Latin scripts (e.g. Hindi, Tamil, Arabic), apostrophes and quotes (`O'Brien`), and leading or trailing spaces.
- [ ] 🟡 **Dates and times are right for the user:** time zones (including users in a different time zone from the server), daylight saving, midnight and month-end, and date formats for the region.
- [ ] 🟡 **Lists behave at the edges:** zero, one and many items. The last page of pagination, sorting, filtering and search with no results all work.
- [ ] 🟡 **Limits are explained:** hitting a plan limit, quota, file size or character limit shows a clear message and what to do, never a silent failure.
- [ ] 🟡 **Files at the edges:** the largest allowed upload, wrong file types, an interrupted upload and duplicate file names.

### Sessions and journeys
- [ ] 🔴 **Smoke test of every main journey, for every user role** (e.g. visitor, free user, paid user, admin, parent/child): sign up, log in, the core action, pay, cancel, log out, delete account. Each step works and shows the right screens.
- [ ] 🟡 **Session expiry mid-task:** if the session expires while someone is filling a form or paying, they're asked to sign in again without losing their work or being charged twice.
- [ ] 🟡 **Multiple tabs and devices:** logging out, changing plan or editing data in one tab or device is reflected in others, with no stale or conflicting saves.
- [ ] 🟡 **Role changes take effect immediately:** downgraded, removed or banned users lose access straight away, including in open sessions.
- [ ] 🟡 **Notifications and emails** for each journey (welcome, receipt, password reset, invite, alerts) are sent once, arrive, show the right details and link to working pages.

<a id="legal-privacy"></a>

## Legal, privacy and compliance

**Flag; don't decide.** You are not a lawyer. Check what exists in the code and site, compare it with the data map from Step 1, and send anything that depends on jurisdiction, money, health or children to the "Needs a lawyer" list. Name the likely laws (GDPR/UK GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, India DPDP Act, COPPA, PIPEDA, LGPD and so on) based on where users are. Ask the user if you don't know.

### Policies and consent

- [ ] 🔴 **Privacy policy** exists, is linked in the footer and at signup, and **matches the data map**: what is collected, why, legal basis, which third parties (hosting, analytics, payments, email, AI providers), where it is stored, how long it's kept, and how to contact you.
- [ ] 🔴 **Terms of service** cover acceptable use, account termination, payment terms, liability limits, governing law and a contact address.
- [ ] 🔴 **Cookie and tracker consent** where required (EU/UK, and increasingly elsewhere). **No non-essential analytics, ads pixels or session replay load before consent.** Verify this in code: look for GA, GTM, Meta Pixel, Hotjar, PostHog or Clarity initialized unconditionally.
- [ ] 🔴 **Consent on forms that collect personal data:** a clear notice plus a link to the privacy policy. Marketing opt-in is unticked by default and separate from the terms.
- [ ] 🟡 **Cookie policy** lists every cookie and tracker and its purpose.
- [ ] 🟡 **Do Not Sell/Share link and Global Privacy Control** support if California users are in scope and the app shares data for ads.

### User data rights

- [ ] 🔴 **Data collected is explained** in plain words: a list of fields.
- [ ] 🔴 **Purpose for each field.** If there's no purpose, recommend not collecting it (data minimization).
- [ ] 🔴 **Account and data deletion** that users can trigger themselves or request easily. It covers the DB, file storage, logs where feasible, and third parties (payments, email lists, analytics, AI provider logs). App stores require in-app account deletion.
- [ ] 🟡 **Data export** (a "download my data" option).
- [ ] 🟡 **Data correction** (users can edit their profile data).
- [ ] 🟡 **Retention rules.** Old data, logs and backups expire on a schedule.
- [ ] 🟡 **Breach plan.** Who to notify and how fast (GDPR: 72 hours to the regulator. India DPDP: notify the Board and affected users).

### Third parties and licensing

- [ ] 🔴 **Every third-party API's terms checked:** commercial use allowed, caching and storage limits, attribution, rate limits, and whether you can resell or redistribute the data. Scraping targets' terms checked.
- [ ] 🔴 **Open-source licenses scanned** (`npx license-checker --summary`, `pip-licenses`, FOSSA, Snyk). Flag GPL, AGPL, SSPL or "non-commercial" licenses in closed-source or SaaS code.
- [ ] 🔴 **No unlicensed copyrighted assets:** images, icons, illustrations, music, video, logos, and stock photos without a license.
- [ ] 🔴 **Fonts are licensed for the web.** Google Fonts and other OFL/Apache fonts are fine. A commercial font (Adobe, Monotype, foundry fonts) needs a **web/app license**: a desktop license doesn't cover `@font-face` or embedding in an app. Check every `@font-face`, `next/font` and font file in `public/`.
- [ ] 🟡 **Asset register:** a list (e.g. `CREDITS.md` or `/licenses`) of every third-party image, icon set, font, illustration and audio file, with its source and license. Images AI tools pulled from the web without a source are a FAIL.
- [ ] 🟡 **Attribution given** where licenses require it (CC-BY, MIT/BSD notices, OpenStreetMap, some icon sets).
- [ ] 🟡 **UI kits, templates, themes and fonts** are licensed for this use (many paid kits forbid SaaS or resale use).
- [ ] 🟡 **AI-generated content.** Check the AI provider's output terms. Generated images or text don't imitate real brands, trademarks, real people or copyrighted characters.
- [ ] 🟡 **Trademark check:** the app name and logo don't clash with an existing brand in the same space.

### Money

- [ ] 🔴 **Refund and cancellation policy** if you charge. Cancelling is as easy as signing up (FTC click-to-cancel style rules, app store rules).
- [ ] 🔴 **Card data never touches your servers.** Use hosted checkout or elements (Stripe, Razorpay, Paddle, Lemon Squeezy) to stay out of PCI scope.
- [ ] 🔴 **Prices shown include or clearly state taxes and recurring charges.** Trials say when billing starts.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tax.** GST/VAT/sales tax registration and invoices. Consider a merchant of record (Paddle, Lemon Squeezy) if unsure. → lawyer or accountant.
- [ ] 🟡 **Business details shown** where required: legal entity name, address and contact (EU, India e-commerce rules).

### Product-specific

- [ ] 🟡 **Age restrictions.** COPPA (US, under 13), GDPR (under 13–16 depending on country), India DPDP (under 18 needs verifiable parental consent; no tracking or targeted ads to children). Age gate if needed.
- [ ] 🟡 **Disclaimers** for health, fitness, finance, investment, legal or AI-generated advice.
- [ ] 🟡 **Takedown process for user content:** a report and takedown process, a DMCA or copyright contact, and moderation. In India, intermediary rules can require a grievance officer. → lawyer.
- [ ] 🟡 **Regulated data or industries:** health (HIPAA), finance, education (FERPA), kids, crypto or gambling. → lawyer before launch.
- [ ] 🟡 **Accessibility law.** ADA (US), the European Accessibility Act (EU, in force from June 2025) and similar may apply. See the **launch-ux** section.

### Honesty

- [ ] 🔴 **Marketing claims are true:** no fake testimonials, logos, user counts, reviews or "as seen in". No "SOC 2 / HIPAA / GDPR compliant", "bank-grade security" or "end-to-end encrypted" unless it's actually true and can be backed up.
- [ ] 🔴 **No dark patterns:** no pre-ticked boxes, hidden fees, fake countdown timers, confirmshaming, or hard-to-find cancel buttons.

### Final legal pass

- [ ] 🔴 **List everything for a lawyer**, with the specific question to ask, the jurisdiction, and the relevant page or file.

<a id="launch-ux"></a>

## UX, launch basics, email and abuse

Mark each item PASS / FAIL / N/A / CAN'T TELL, with evidence. 🔴 blocks launch · 🟡 before scale · 🟢 recommended.

### Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA)

- [ ] 🔴 **Real controls:** `<button>` and `<a>` instead of clickable `<div>`s, and every input has a `<label>`.
- [ ] 🔴 **Keyboard works everywhere**, with a visible focus state and no keyboard traps in modals or menus.
- [ ] 🔴 **Alt text** on meaningful images, and empty `alt=""` on decorative ones.
- [ ] 🟡 **Colour contrast** at least 4.5:1 for text, and colour isn't the only signal (for example, red borders on errors also need text).
- [ ] 🟡 **Forms announce errors** next to the field, with text.
- [ ] 🟡 **Respects `prefers-reduced-motion`**, and nothing flashes.
- [ ] 🟢 **Run axe or Lighthouse accessibility** and fix serious issues.

### Responsive and performance

- [ ] 🔴 **Works on a phone.** Check key pages at 375px wide: no horizontal scroll, tap targets at least 44px, and modals fit on screen.
- [ ] 🟡 **Performance budget:** LCP under 2.5s, INP under 200ms, CLS under 0.1 on mobile (Lighthouse or PageSpeed Insights).
- [ ] 🟡 **JS bundle size** is reasonable. Big libraries and below-the-fold content load lazily.
- [ ] 🟡 **Images** are optimized and sized, and fonts don't block rendering.

### Launch basics

- [ ] 🔴 **Custom 404 and 500 pages** that don't leak stack traces.
- [ ] 🔴 **Empty, loading and error states** for every screen that loads data.
- [ ] 🔴 **A support contact** (email or form) is visible.
- [ ] 🟡 **Staging and preview sites aren't indexed** (`noindex` or password protection). Production isn't accidentally `noindex`.
- [ ] 🟡 **SEO basics:** a unique `<title>` and meta description per page, Open Graph and social images, `sitemap.xml`, `robots.txt`, canonical URLs, and a favicon.
- [ ] 🟡 **Analytics** for key events (signup, activation, payment), installed in a consent-respecting way.
- [ ] 🟡 **Browser support:** tested in Chrome, Safari (including iOS Safari) and Firefox.
- [ ] 🟢 **Changelog or status page** so users know what changed.

### Email

- [ ] 🔴 **Sending domain authenticated:** SPF, DKIM and DMARC set (otherwise mail lands in spam, and Gmail and Yahoo require it for bulk senders).
- [ ] 🔴 **Transactional email** (verification, reset, receipts) uses a real provider (Resend, Postmark, SES, SendGrid), not a personal Gmail account.
- [ ] 🔴 **Marketing email** has opt-in consent, a one-click unsubscribe and a physical address (CAN-SPAM and GDPR rules).
- [ ] 🟡 **Bounces and complaints** are handled. Emails that bounce stop being sent to.
- [ ] 🟡 **No user-controlled content in emails** that could make them phishing tools (for example, the name field in "X invited you").

### Abuse and fraud

- [ ] 🔴 **Signup abuse:** rate limits and bot protection. Disposable-email blocking if free tiers are valuable.
- [ ] 🔴 **SMS and OTP pumping:** rate limits per number and IP, and country restrictions if you only serve some countries.
- [ ] 🟡 **Free-tier and trial abuse:** limits per account, device or card. Watch for credit farming.
- [ ] 🟡 **User-generated content:** report, block and moderation tools. Uploaded files can't be used to host malware or phishing on your domain.
- [ ] 🟡 **Invites and sharing links** expire and can be revoked. Share links aren't guessable.

### Mobile apps

Mobile apps have their own module: see the **mobile** section (store requirements, permission strings, privacy manifests, secure token storage, deep links, crash reporting).

<a id="platforms"></a>

## Platform-specific traps

Check the sections for the stack you found in Step 1. These are the problems that come up again and again in AI-built apps on each platform.

### Supabase

- [ ] 🔴 RLS is **enabled on every table** in exposed schemas (`select relname, relrowsecurity from pg_class join pg_namespace n on n.oid = relnamespace where nspname = 'public' and relkind = 'r';`).
- [ ] 🔴 No policy uses `using (true)` or `with check (true)` on user data. Policies use `auth.uid()` and have a `WITH CHECK` on insert and update.
- [ ] 🔴 The `service_role` key is used only in server code or edge functions, never in the client or `NEXT_PUBLIC_`/`VITE_` variables. (The anon key in the client is fine *only* because RLS protects the data.)
- [ ] 🔴 Storage buckets holding private files are **not public**, and storage policies check ownership.
- [ ] 🔴 `security definer` functions and RPCs check the caller's permissions, set `search_path`, and aren't callable by `anon` unless intended.
- [ ] 🔴 Views don't bypass RLS (use `security_invoker = true` on Postgres 15+).
- [ ] 🟡 The Supabase **Security Advisor and Performance Advisor** in the dashboard show no errors.
- [ ] 🟡 Auth settings: email confirmation on, a sensible OTP expiry, leaked-password protection, redirect URLs restricted, and the rate limits reviewed.
- [ ] 🟡 Using the connection pooler for serverless. Point-in-time recovery or backups on the right plan.
- [ ] 🟡 Edge functions verify the JWT unless they're deliberately public (webhooks verify signatures instead).

### Firebase / Firestore

- [ ] 🔴 Firestore, Realtime Database and Storage rules are not in test mode (`allow read, write: if true` or a date-based rule that expires).
- [ ] 🔴 Rules check `request.auth.uid` against document ownership and validate the fields written (users can't set `role` or `isAdmin`).
- [ ] 🟡 App Check is enabled to cut down abuse of the public config.
- [ ] 🟡 Cloud Functions validate auth and input. Admin SDK use is restricted to trusted code.
- [ ] 🟡 Budget alerts are set. Firestore reads can explode in cost with unbounded listeners.

### Vercel / Netlify / Cloudflare Pages

- [ ] 🔴 **Preview deployments** don't use production DBs or live payment keys, and are protected (Vercel Deployment Protection) if they expose real data.
- [ ] 🔴 Environment variables are scoped per environment (Production / Preview / Development).
- [ ] 🟡 Serverless function timeouts and regions are set, with spend management or limits on.
- [ ] 🟡 Production source maps aren't publicly downloadable, or that's a deliberate decision.

### Cloudflare (Workers, Pages, D1, R2)

- [ ] 🔴 **Always Use HTTPS** is on, and the **minimum TLS version is 1.2** (SSL/TLS → Edge Certificates). Check live: `curl -I http://<domain>` should redirect, and `curl --tlsv1.1 --tls-max 1.1 https://<domain>` should fail.
- [ ] 🔴 **`*.workers.dev` and `*.pages.dev` routes** are disabled for production Workers, or redirected to the real domain (they bypass custom-domain rules and may expose a personal account name).
- [ ] 🔴 Secrets are set with `wrangler secret put` or the dashboard, not in `wrangler.toml` `[vars]`.
- [ ] 🔴 R2 buckets with private data have no public access and no `r2.dev` URL. Files are served through signed URLs or the Worker.
- [ ] 🟡 D1 has Time Travel/backups, and a restore has been tested. Migrations are done with `wrangler d1 migrations`.
- [ ] 🟡 The deploy runs the tests first (CI, then `wrangler deploy`), not only deploy-on-push.
- [ ] 🟡 Preview and dev Workers have their own bindings (DB, KV, R2), never production's.

### Next.js

- [ ] 🔴 **Server Actions and Route Handlers each check auth and authorization themselves.** Middleware alone is not enough (and older versions had middleware bypass bugs, so keep Next.js patched).
- [ ] 🔴 No secrets in `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`. Server-only modules import `server-only`.
- [ ] 🔴 Data passed to Client Components doesn't include fields the user shouldn't see (password hashes, other users' emails, internal flags). Use DTOs or `taintObjectReference` where useful.
- [ ] 🟡 `revalidate` and caching don't serve one user's private data to another.
- [ ] 🟡 `next.config` `images.remotePatterns` isn't a wildcard that turns your app into an open image proxy.

### Lovable / Bolt / v0 / Replit Agent / Firebase Studio (browser builders)

- [ ] 🔴 Read the generated Supabase or Firebase rules yourself. These tools often scaffold permissive rules to make the demo work.
- [ ] 🔴 Secrets are in the platform's secrets manager (Replit Secrets, Lovable or Supabase secrets), not in code. If the project or repo is public, assume earlier secrets have leaked and rotate them.
- [ ] 🔴 Payment and AI calls go through a backend or edge function, not straight from the browser.
- [ ] 🔴 The project or repo isn't accidentally **public** with real data or keys.
- [ ] 🟡 Export the code to GitHub so you have history, backups and the ability to run real tooling (`npm audit`, tests).
- [ ] 🟡 A custom domain with HTTPS, and the platform's default subdomain redirects or is noindexed.

### Stripe / Razorpay / Paddle / Lemon Squeezy

- [ ] 🔴 Webhook signatures are verified with the endpoint secret, using the **raw** request body.
- [ ] 🔴 Price IDs or amounts come from the server. Never trust an amount sent by the client.
- [ ] 🔴 Access is granted from webhook events (`checkout.session.completed`, `invoice.paid`, `payment.captured`), and revoked on `customer.subscription.deleted`, failed payments and refunds.
- [ ] 🔴 Live keys only in production. Test mode was used to rehearse success, failure, refund, dispute and cancellation.
- [ ] 🟡 Customer portal or self-serve cancellation.

### Express / Fastify / Django / Flask / Rails / Laravel

- [ ] 🔴 Debug mode is off in production (`DEBUG=False`, `app.debug=False`, `APP_DEBUG=false`, `NODE_ENV=production`).
- [ ] 🔴 The framework's secret key or session secret is strong, unique per environment, and not committed.
- [ ] 🔴 `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, CSRF middleware and secure-cookie settings are configured.
- [ ] 🟡 `helmet` (Express) or the framework's security middleware is on. The body size limit is set.
- [ ] 🟡 The admin panel (Django admin, Rails admin and so on) is at a non-default path, with strong auth and 2FA.

### Expo / React Native / Flutter

- See the **mobile** section. Plus: `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` variables are visible to anyone, so never put secrets there.

### Docker / VPS / self-hosted

- [ ] 🔴 Database, Redis and admin ports aren't open to the internet. A firewall allows only 80, 443 and SSH (key-only, no root login).
- [ ] 🔴 Containers don't run as root where avoidable, and no secrets are baked into images (`docker history`).
- [ ] 🟡 OS and images are updated regularly. Unattended security upgrades are on.
- [ ] 🟡 Backups are stored off the server.

<a id="lawsuits"></a>

## Lawsuit magnets and jurisdictions

Load this file for every Full and Enterprise check of an app with real users. It covers the **specific, easy-to-detect website and app basics that people actually get sued, fined or sent demand letters over**. Many are hunted by automated crawlers, so small apps are targets too.

**Rules:** you are not a lawyer. Detect the trap in the code or on the live site, cite the evidence, and put the legal judgement on the "Needs a lawyer" list. What applies depends on **where the users are**, not where the business is. Use the regions from Step 1, and ask if they're unknown. Never tell the user they're "compliant".

### Detect in the code (every app)

- [ ] 🔴 **Third-party fonts loaded from outside servers:** Google Fonts or other font CDNs loaded from `fonts.googleapis.com`, `fonts.gstatic.com`, `use.typekit.net` or similar send every visitor's IP address to that company. In Germany and Austria this triggered a wave of automated demand letters (€100–€500 each) after a 2022 Munich court ruling. **Fix:** self-host the font files (`next/font` self-hosts Google Fonts by default; otherwise download them into `public/`). Grep CSS, HTML, `<link>` tags and `@import`.
- [ ] 🔴 **Trackers fire before consent:** analytics, ad pixels (Meta, TikTok, Google Ads, LinkedIn), session replay (Hotjar, Clarity, FullStory, LogRocket), heatmaps and live chat or chatbot widgets must not load until the user agrees (where consent is required). In California, thousands of wiretap (CIPA) lawsuits target exactly these tools, with statutory damages claimed per visitor. Check script tags, tag managers and SDK init calls for consent gating.
- [ ] 🔴 **Third-party embeds load before consent:** YouTube, Vimeo, Google Maps, social media embeds, reCAPTCHA and similar also send visitor data. Use click-to-load placeholders or privacy-enhanced modes (e.g. `youtube-nocookie.com`), and disclose them.
- [ ] 🔴 **Chat and session replay capture sensitive input:** chat widgets, session replay and form analytics must not record passwords, payment, health or children's data. Inputs are masked, and the tool is disclosed in the privacy policy.
- [ ] 🔴 **Accessibility basics on revenue paths:** sign-up, login, product pages, cart, checkout and booking work with keyboard only and a screen reader (labels, alt text, focus, contrast). Thousands of US ADA website lawsuits are filed every year, most against small online businesses. **Accessibility overlay widgets don't prevent lawsuits** (many suits target sites using them). Flag any overlay used *instead of* real fixes.
- [ ] 🔴 **Subscriptions and auto-renewal:** before charging, clearly show price, billing frequency, renewal terms and how to cancel, next to the "subscribe" button. Get **separate, affirmative consent** (no pre-ticked box), keep proof of consent, send renewal reminders, and offer **online cancellation that's as easy as sign-up** (a visible "cancel" link, with no forced phone calls or endless retention screens). This is required in California for its residents, and similar rules exist elsewhere.
- [ ] 🔴 **Marketing email and SMS have proof of opt-in:** consent is separate from the terms, unticked by default, recorded (who, when, how, what wording), and every message has a working one-click unsubscribe. Canada (CASL) requires express opt-in for most commercial messages, with large fines. US texts are covered by TCPA rules.
- [ ] 🔴 **No fake urgency, fake reviews or hidden fees:** countdown timers that reset, "only 2 left" that isn't true, made-up testimonials, drip pricing (fees added at checkout) and pre-ticked add-ons attract regulator action in the US, EU, UK, India and elsewhere.
- [ ] 🟡 **Cookie banner is fair:** "Reject all" is as easy as "Accept all" (same layer, same prominence) for EU and UK users, choices are remembered, and consent can be withdrawn later. Global Privacy Control (GPC) signals are honoured for California users.
- [ ] 🟡 **Legal notice / Impressum** if the app targets users in Germany, Austria or Switzerland, or makes money there (even through ads or affiliate links): the legal name, address, contact email and phone, company register and VAT ID where applicable, reachable from every page in **two clicks or fewer** (usually the footer). Missing or hidden notices lead to warning letters and fines.
- [ ] 🟡 **French for Quebec users:** if the app sells to or serves people in Quebec, the website, consent banner, privacy notice and commercial content must be available in French (Bill 96, Law 25).
- [ ] 🟡 **Biometric data** (face scans, face filters, voice prints, fingerprints): explicit written consent, a published retention and destruction policy, and no selling. Illinois (BIPA) allows private lawsuits per person, and other states and the EU treat it as sensitive data.
- [ ] 🟡 **Video viewing data shared with ad pixels:** if the app shows videos and sends what a user watched to Meta or other ad platforms, US Video Privacy Protection Act lawsuits are a known risk. Don't send video titles or URLs to pixels without consent.
- [ ] 🟡 **Children:** apps directed at children, or that know they have child users, follow COPPA (US, under 13), GDPR-K, the UK Children's Code and India DPDP (under 18). See the **kids-education** section.

### Country and region table

Use the regions from Step 1. Each row lists the traps to check for users in that place. Mark each row that applies in the report, and send judgement calls to the lawyer list.

| Users in | Main laws to name | Traps to check |
|---|---|---|
| **European Union** | GDPR, ePrivacy (cookies), Digital Services Act (platforms), European Accessibility Act (many consumer services since June 2025), consumer rights rules | Self-hosted fonts and consent before trackers; fair cookie banner; privacy policy with legal basis and transfers outside the EU; 14-day withdrawal right and clear pricing for online sales; accessibility for e-commerce and banking-type services; notice-and-action and transparency duties for platforms with user content |
| **Germany, Austria, Switzerland** | GDPR, DDG §5 (Impressum), UWG (competition warning letters), Swiss FADP | Impressum within two clicks; Google Fonts and other IP leaks; correct cookie consent; complete and accurate privacy policy (competitors can send warning letters) |
| **United Kingdom** | UK GDPR, PECR (cookies, marketing), Online Safety Act (user-to-user and search services), Children's Code | Cookie consent with fair reject; marketing opt-in; risk assessments and reporting tools if users can share content or talk to each other; age-appropriate design for child users |
| **United States: California** | CCPA/CPRA, CIPA (wiretap), Automatic Renewal Law, CalOPPA | "Do Not Sell or Share" link and GPC support where data is shared for ads; no pixels, replay or chat before consent; auto-renewal consent, reminders and click-to-cancel; privacy policy with a California section |
| **United States: all** | ADA (accessibility), COPPA, TCPA (texts and calls), CAN-SPAM, ROSCA (online subscriptions), FTC Act (deceptive practices), state privacy laws (Virginia, Colorado, Texas and others), Illinois BIPA, VPPA | Accessibility on revenue paths; children under 13; SMS consent; email unsubscribe and address; subscription disclosures; honest marketing and reviews; biometric consent; video data in pixels |
| **Canada** | PIPEDA, Quebec Law 25, Quebec Bill 96 (French), CASL, Ontario AODA (accessibility) | Opt-in before tracking for Quebec users; French versions for Quebec; express consent for marketing messages; privacy officer contact; accessibility for larger Ontario organisations |
| **India** | DPDP Act and Rules, IT Act and IT Rules (intermediaries), Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, CERT-In directions, RBI rules for payments | Notice and consent in clear language; verifiable parental consent for under-18s; grievance officer for platforms; seller and price disclosures for e-commerce; incident reporting within 6 hours to CERT-In; card and recurring-payment rules |
| **Brazil** | LGPD, Consumer Defense Code | Legal basis and data protection officer contact; consumer rights for online sales, including a 7-day withdrawal |
| **Australia** | Privacy Act, Spam Act, Australian Consumer Law | Privacy policy, consented marketing with unsubscribe, honest pricing and no misleading claims |
| **Japan, South Korea, Singapore and others** | APPI (Japan), PIPA (Korea), PDPA (Singapore) | Purpose notice, consent for sharing, cross-border transfer notices |
| **Global (app stores)** | Apple App Store and Google Play policies | Account deletion in the app, privacy labels that match the app, in-app purchase rules (see the **mobile** section) |

### Report it this way
- Put the code-detectable traps (fonts, trackers before consent, embeds, overlays, missing cancel link, missing Impressum) under 🔴 or 🟡 like any other finding, each with a fix prompt. Most are **safe to auto-fix** (self-hosting fonts, gating scripts behind consent), except changes users will see.
- Put the region rows that apply under **Needs a lawyer**, phrased as questions: *"We have users in Quebec: do our privacy notice and checkout need French versions?"*

<a id="supply-chain"></a>

## Supply chain and CI security

Load this file for every Full and Enterprise check. Attackers increasingly get in through **dependencies, build pipelines and third-party scripts** rather than the app's own code. Check `package.json` and lockfiles, `.github/workflows/`, other CI configs, and every `<script src>` loaded from another domain.

### Dependencies
- [ ] 🔴 **Lockfile committed and used:** `package-lock.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn.lock`, `poetry.lock` or `requirements.txt` with pinned versions. CI installs with `npm ci` / `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` (not a plain `npm install` that can drift).
- [ ] 🔴 **No suspicious or unknown packages:** every dependency is real, widely used and maintained. Check for typosquats (e.g. `reqeusts`, `lodahs`), very new packages with few downloads, and packages added by AI tools that don't match their purpose.
- [ ] 🟡 **Install scripts reviewed:** dependencies with `preinstall`/`postinstall` scripts are known and needed. Consider `npm config set ignore-scripts true` for CI where possible, or an allow-list.
- [ ] 🟡 **No dependencies from Git URLs, tarballs or personal forks** unless intentional and pinned to a commit.
- [ ] 🟡 **Unused dependencies removed** (`npx depcheck` or similar): fewer packages, fewer risks.
- [ ] 🟡 **Private package scopes protected:** internal package names are scoped (`@company/…`) and registries configured, so a public package with the same name can't be installed instead (dependency confusion).

### CI/CD pipelines (GitHub Actions and similar)
- [ ] 🔴 **Third-party actions pinned to a full commit SHA** (not `@main` or `@v1`), at least for actions that touch secrets or deploy.
- [ ] 🔴 **No dangerous triggers:** `pull_request_target` or `workflow_run` workflows never check out and run code from a fork with access to secrets.
- [ ] 🔴 **Least-privilege tokens:** `permissions:` set per workflow (default `contents: read`). Deploy tokens are scoped to one project, and short-lived OIDC credentials are used for cloud deploys where possible.
- [ ] 🔴 **Secrets never printed or exposed:** no `echo $SECRET`, no secrets in build logs, artifacts, caches or client bundles. Secrets aren't available to pull requests from forks.
- [ ] 🟡 **No untrusted input in shell steps:** PR titles, branch names and issue text aren't interpolated directly into `run:` commands (`${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}`). Pass them through environment variables instead.
- [ ] 🟡 **Production deploys need the main branch and passing checks.** Environment protection rules or required reviewers for production.
- [ ] 🟢 **Build provenance / signed artifacts** for packages or containers you publish.

### Third-party scripts in the browser
- [ ] 🔴 **Every external script is known and needed:** list each `<script src>` from another domain (analytics, chat, A/B testing, tag managers, widgets). Each one can read the page, including forms and tokens.
- [ ] 🟡 **Subresource Integrity (SRI)** (`integrity="sha384-…"` plus `crossorigin`) on scripts and styles loaded from public CDNs with fixed versions, or self-host them.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tag managers are locked down:** few people can publish tags, and changes are reviewed. A tag manager can inject any script into production.
- [ ] 🟡 **Script sources restricted by CSP:** the Content Security Policy limits which domains scripts can load from (see the **security** section).
- [ ] 🟡 **Third-party scripts don't load on sensitive pages** (login, payment, password reset, admin), or are reviewed for those pages.

### Repository and accounts
- [ ] 🔴 **Secret scanning and push protection are on** for the repository.
- [ ] 🟡 **Branch protection and required reviews** on the default branch, and `CODEOWNERS` for sensitive paths (auth, payments, infra, workflows).
- [ ] 🟡 **Signed commits or verified committers** for maintainers, and 2FA required for the organisation.
- [ ] 🟢 **An SBOM can be generated** (e.g. `npx @cyclonedx/cyclonedx-npm`) for customers or audits.

<a id="maintainability"></a>

## Maintainability and handover

Load this file for every Full and Enterprise check. AI-built apps often work but can't be maintained, handed to a developer, sold or shown to investors without a rewrite. These checks make sure someone other than the original builder (or their AI) can safely run and change the app. Most items are 🟡 or 🟢: they rarely block a launch, but they decide how expensive every future change is.

### Code health
- [ ] 🟡 **Type safety:** TypeScript `strict` mode (or the language's equivalent), with few `any`, `@ts-ignore` or `eslint-disable` escapes. Each escape has a reason.
- [ ] 🟡 **Linting and formatting run automatically** (ESLint/Biome/Ruff and Prettier, or similar), in CI and on commit.
- [ ] 🟡 **No dead or duplicated code:** unused files, components, routes and exports removed. No copy-pasted versions of the same logic from different AI sessions (see blind spot 16).
- [ ] 🟡 **No giant files or functions:** files over ~500 lines or functions over ~80 lines doing many things are listed for future splitting (list them; don't refactor during the audit).
- [ ] 🟡 **Configuration isn't hard-coded:** URLs, prices, limits, feature switches and email addresses live in config or env vars, not scattered through the code.
- [ ] 🟢 **Consistent structure and naming** that a new developer can guess (where routes, components, server code and tests live).

### Documentation
- [ ] 🔴 **README explains how to run it:** install, required env vars (with an `.env.example` listing every variable and what it's for, no real values), how to run locally, run tests, and deploy.
- [ ] 🟡 **Architecture notes:** a short page or diagram showing the main parts (frontend, API, database, storage, third-party services) and how data flows.
- [ ] 🟡 **Project rules for AI tools** (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursor/rules`): stack, conventions, "never do" rules, and how to test, so future AI sessions don't drift.
- [ ] 🟡 **Database schema is documented** through migrations and a short description of the main tables and relationships.
- [ ] 🟢 **Changelog or release notes** for user-visible changes.

### Ownership and handover
- [ ] 🔴 **The business owns every account:** domain, hosting, database, code repository, app store accounts, payment provider, email provider and AI providers are owned by the company (or the client, for agency work), not a freelancer's or employee's personal login. → ask the user.
- [ ] 🔴 **Credentials are in a password manager or vault** that more than one trusted person can access. No credentials only in someone's head or chat history.
- [ ] 🟡 **Licences and third-party terms are listed** for code, assets, fonts, APIs and AI models (see the **legal-privacy** section), so a buyer or investor can check them.
- [ ] 🟡 **Known issues and tech debt are written down,** including shortcuts taken during the build and the latest vibe-check report.
- [ ] 🟢 **Due-diligence pack ready:** architecture notes, security summary, the latest vibe-check report, dependency and licence list, data map, and the policies in the **policies** section.

<a id="ai-features"></a>

## AI and LLM features

Use this file only if the app calls an LLM or AI API (chatbots, generation, agents, RAG, AI image or voice). Mark each item PASS / FAIL / N/A / CAN'T TELL, with evidence.

### Keys and cost

- [ ] 🔴 **AI provider keys stay on the server.** No OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini or Replicate key in client code or a public env var. The browser calls your backend, never the provider directly.
- [ ] 🔴 **Per-user limits:** rate limits, daily or monthly quotas, `max_tokens` on every call, and a limit on input size (characters, file size, pages).
- [ ] 🔴 **Spend cap and billing alert** set at the provider (ask the user).
- [ ] 🔴 **Anonymous users can't run up the bill.** Unauthenticated AI endpoints get strict limits or CAPTCHA.
- [ ] 🟡 **Calls are triggered deliberately:** not on every keystroke, render or retry loop. Debounce and cancel in-flight requests.
- [ ] 🟡 **Streaming and long calls** have timeouts and handle user cancellation.
- [ ] 🟢 **Cache** repeated identical prompts where that's safe.

### Prompt injection and output handling

- [ ] 🔴 **LLM output is treated as untrusted input.** It is never rendered as raw HTML (sanitize markdown output), never run as code or SQL, and never used as a URL, file path or shell argument without validation.
- [ ] 🔴 **Prompt injection is assumed.** Text from users, uploaded documents, web pages, emails or RAG sources can contain instructions. The model's permissions must be safe even if it obeys them.
- [ ] 🔴 **Tool and agent permissions are least-privilege.** Tools run with the *end user's* permissions (not admin or service-role). Destructive or costly actions (sending email, payments, deleting, posting) need explicit user confirmation.
- [ ] 🔴 **No data leaks across users.** RAG retrieval and conversation history are filtered by the current user or tenant on the server, not by asking the model.
- [ ] 🟡 **System prompts contain no secrets.** Assume users can extract the system prompt.
- [ ] 🟡 **Structured output is validated** against a schema before use.
- [ ] 🟡 **No exfiltration through rendered markdown.** Images and links in model output can't send data to attacker URLs (restrict image domains or strip external images).

### Privacy, safety and honesty

- [ ] 🔴 **The privacy policy names the AI providers** and says what data is sent to them. Check the provider's data-retention and training terms (opt out of training where possible).
- [ ] 🔴 **No sensitive data sent unnecessarily:** health records, government IDs, passwords and payment data are stripped before prompts.
- [ ] 🟡 **Content moderation** on user prompts and on outputs if the app is public or generates images, so it can't be used to produce harmful content under your brand.
- [ ] 🟡 **AI disclosure.** Users know they're talking to AI. AI-generated content is labelled where laws or platforms require it (EU AI Act transparency rules, app store policies).
- [ ] 🟡 **High-stakes outputs** (health, legal, finance, safety) carry disclaimers and a path to a human.
- [ ] 🟡 **Hallucination handling.** Citations or sources where accuracy matters, and the UI doesn't present guesses as facts.
- [ ] 🟡 **Logs of prompts and outputs** are access-controlled, have a retention limit, and are covered by deletion requests.
- [ ] 🟢 **Evals.** A small set of test prompts (including jailbreak and injection attempts) run before changing models or prompts.
- [ ] 🟢 **Fallback** when the provider is down or rate-limits you, so users see a clear message instead of a hang.

<a id="ai-agents"></a>

## AI agents, tools and MCP servers

Load this file when the app **lets an AI take actions**: an agent that calls tools or APIs, browses, writes to a database, sends messages or spends money, or when the project **is or includes an MCP server, plugin or tool provider** for AI assistants. This is on top of the **ai-features** section, which covers basic LLM use (keys, cost, prompt injection, output handling).

### Tools and permissions
- [ ] 🔴 **Least-privilege tools:** each tool does one specific thing with tightly scoped inputs. No generic "run SQL", "run shell command", "fetch any URL" or "call any API" tools exposed to the model unless sandboxed.
- [ ] 🔴 **Tools act as the end user, not as admin:** every tool call is authorized with the current user's permissions and tenant. The service key or admin token is never what the model uses.
- [ ] 🔴 **Human confirmation for risky actions:** sending email or messages, payments or refunds, deleting data, changing permissions, publishing content and anything irreversible needs explicit user approval in the UI, showing exactly what will happen.
- [ ] 🔴 **Indirect prompt injection is assumed:** content the agent reads (web pages, emails, documents, tickets, database rows, tool results) can contain instructions. The agent's permissions must be safe even if it follows them. Untrusted content is never allowed to choose recipients, URLs or amounts for risky actions without confirmation.
- [ ] 🔴 **No data exfiltration paths:** the agent can't send private data to arbitrary URLs (through fetch tools, rendered images or links, webhooks or email). Outbound destinations are allow-listed.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tool inputs are validated with a schema** on the server, and tool outputs are size-limited and treated as untrusted.
- [ ] 🟡 **Sandboxing for code execution or browsing:** isolated containers or VMs with no access to production secrets or internal networks, plus time, memory and network limits.

### Limits and cost
- [ ] 🔴 **Loop and cost limits:** maximum steps, tool calls, tokens, time and spend per task and per user, with the agent stopping cleanly when a limit is hit.
- [ ] 🟡 **Rate limits per user** on agent runs and expensive tools.
- [ ] 🟡 **Idempotent actions:** retries or repeated agent steps can't double-send, double-charge or create duplicates (idempotency keys on actions).

### Visibility and control
- [ ] 🔴 **Audit log of agent actions:** which user, which tool, inputs (secrets masked), result and time, viewable by the user and admins.
- [ ] 🟡 **Kill switch:** agents or specific tools can be disabled instantly without a deploy.
- [ ] 🟡 **Users can see and undo** what the agent did where possible (drafts before sending, version history).
- [ ] 🟡 **Evaluation set** of normal tasks plus injection and jailbreak attempts, run before changing models, prompts or tools.

### MCP servers, plugins and tool providers
- [ ] 🔴 **Authentication on remote MCP endpoints:** OAuth (with PKCE) or scoped API keys. No unauthenticated remote MCP servers that touch user data. Tokens are scoped per user and revocable.
- [ ] 🔴 **Local MCP servers run with minimal access:** only the folders, commands and network access they need. No shell or filesystem access beyond the stated purpose.
- [ ] 🔴 **Tool descriptions are honest and minimal:** descriptions don't contain instructions aimed at the AI ("always call this tool first", "ignore other tools"), and don't change unexpectedly after users approve them.
- [ ] 🟡 **Destructive tools are clearly marked** (read-only vs write) so clients can ask for confirmation, and they support a dry-run where possible.
- [ ] 🟡 **Data returned to the AI is minimal:** no secrets, internal IDs or other users' data in tool results. Large results are paginated.
- [ ] 🟡 **Published packages are signed and pinned:** users install a known version, and updates are reviewed (a malicious update to a tool server reaches every user).

<a id="kids-education"></a>

## Children, students and education

Load this file when anyone under 18 can use the app, or when it serves schools, colleges or students. Children's data carries the strictest obligations, and the biggest consequences if it leaks. Flag every legal item for a lawyer, and for the institution if a school, college or government body runs the program.

**Laws to name, depending on where users are:** India DPDP Act (under 18 = child: verifiable parental consent, no tracking, no behavioural monitoring, no targeted ads). US COPPA (under 13), FERPA (school records), and state student-privacy laws. EU/UK GDPR (age of consent 13–16 by country) and the UK Age-Appropriate Design Code.

### Age and consent
- [ ] 🔴 **The age or school level is known.** The app can tell whether a user is a minor (date of birth, class or grade, or the institution says so).
- [ ] 🔴 **Verifiable parental or guardian consent** before collecting a minor's personal data, where the law requires it. **A checkbox the child ticks is not verifiable consent.** The decision on the method belongs to the institution and its lawyer; flag it rather than inventing one.
- [ ] 🔴 **Consent is recorded:** who consented, for which child, when, how, and which version of the notice.
- [ ] 🟡 **Consent can be withdrawn**, and withdrawing it triggers deletion.
- [ ] 🟡 **The notice for children and parents** uses plain language, in the users' languages.

### Data minimization and retention
- [ ] 🔴 **Only necessary data is collected.** No extra fields "for later". Every field in the data map has a purpose.
- [ ] 🔴 **Retention limits exist and are enforced:** photos, documents and records of minors are deleted or anonymized on a schedule (e.g. X days after the event or term). **Keeping minors' data forever is a 🔴.**
- [ ] 🔴 **Photos and media:** EXIF and GPS metadata is stripped unless needed and disclosed. Faces of minors aren't public. Access to media is logged.
- [ ] 🟡 **Pseudonymization is described accurately:** hashed or coded IDs and phone numbers are described honestly (hashes of phone numbers can often be reversed by brute force; don't claim "cannot be reversed").

### Tracking, ads and profiling
- [ ] 🔴 **No third-party trackers, ad pixels or session replay** on pages minors use.
- [ ] 🔴 **No targeted advertising or behavioural profiling** of minors.
- [ ] 🟡 **Third-party embeds and fonts** (YouTube, Google Fonts, chat widgets) are disclosed. Self-host where possible.

### Access and safety
- [ ] 🔴 **Least-privilege staff access.** Teachers and staff see only their own students. Admin access is limited, logged and reviewed.
- [ ] 🔴 **Students' personal details are never returned** by any public page, API, export or search.
- [ ] 🟡 **No public profiles or open messaging** between adults and minors unless that's the product's purpose, and then with moderation and reporting.
- [ ] 🟡 **Exports** (CSV, PDF) containing student data are restricted, logged and watermarked where possible.

### Institutions and government programs
- [ ] 🟡 **Roles are clear:** who is the data fiduciary or controller (school, district, department), and who is the processor (you). A written agreement exists or is planned.
- [ ] 🟡 **Hosting and certification requirements asked about** (e.g. Indian government programs may need MeitY-empanelled hosting, a CERT-In-empanelled or STQC security audit, and GIGW website compliance). → ask the user.
- [ ] 🟡 **A named grievance officer or contact** is in the privacy policy, with a response timeline.
- [ ] 🟡 **Breach plan for student data:** who notifies the institution, parents and regulator, and how fast.

<a id="health"></a>

## Health, medical, wellness and fitness

Load this file when the app collects health, medical, mental-health, fitness, fertility or biometric data, or gives health advice. Laws to flag for a lawyer: US HIPAA (if you work with covered entities) and state health-data laws (e.g. Washington's My Health My Data Act). EU/UK GDPR, where health data is a "special category" that needs explicit consent. India DPDP Act, plus telemedicine and Digital Health (ABDM) rules where relevant.

- [ ] 🔴 **Explicit, separate consent** for health data, not bundled into the terms.
- [ ] 🔴 **No health data sent to analytics, ad pixels, session replay or marketing tools** (including URL paths and event names that reveal conditions).
- [ ] 🔴 **Encryption at rest and in transit.** Field-level encryption for diagnoses, notes and documents.
- [ ] 🔴 **Strict access control** with audit logs on every read of health records.
- [ ] 🔴 **Medical disclaimer** and an emergency path ("If this is an emergency, call …") wherever advice or AI answers appear.
- [ ] 🔴 **AI features** don't diagnose or prescribe unless the product is approved for that. Output is labelled as AI, with a route to a human professional.
- [ ] 🟡 **A BAA or DPA** with every vendor that touches health data (hosting, email, AI provider). Many default plans don't allow health data.
- [ ] 🟡 **Retention and deletion** rules for health records, including backups and AI provider logs.
- [ ] 🟡 **Notifications** (push, email, SMS) don't reveal health details on lock screens or in subject lines.
- [ ] 🟡 **Regulatory status checked:** does the feature make the app a medical device or software as a medical device (CDSCO in India, FDA in the US, MDR in the EU)? → lawyer.

<a id="fintech"></a>

## Payments, wallets and financial features

Load this file when the app takes payments, holds balances, pays out to users, lends, invests, or handles financial data. Also check the payments items in the **legal-privacy** section and the Stripe/Razorpay section of the **platforms** section.

### Payments
- [ ] 🔴 **No card numbers anywhere in your systems:** logs, DB, analytics, support tools and error tracking. (Hosted checkout is checked in the **legal-privacy** section.)
- [ ] 🔴 **Amounts, currency and tax** are calculated on the server.
- [ ] 🔴 **Webhooks are verified and idempotent.** Entitlements are granted and revoked only from verified events (paid, refunded, disputed, cancelled, failed).
- [ ] 🔴 **Test and live modes are separate.** Failure, refund, dispute and cancel flows were rehearsed in test mode.
- [ ] 🟡 **Reconciliation:** a daily check that your records match the processor's (missed webhooks happen).
- [ ] 🟡 **Invoices and receipts** meet local rules (e.g. GST invoices in India, VAT in the EU). → accountant.
- [ ] 🟡 **Recurring payments** follow local mandate rules (e.g. RBI e-mandate and pre-debit notifications in India).

### Money movement and balances
- [ ] 🔴 **A ledger, not a balance column:** balances come from immutable, double-entry transactions. No `UPDATE balance = …` without a transaction record.
- [ ] 🔴 **Atomic transfers,** with constraints that prevent negative balances and double spends (see the **fixes** section, race conditions).
- [ ] 🔴 **Payouts** need verified identity and bank details, with limits, delays for new accounts, and manual review for unusual amounts.
- [ ] 🟡 **Fraud controls:** velocity limits, device and IP signals, card-testing protection on checkout, alerts for spikes in refunds or disputes.

### Regulation (flag for a lawyer; don't decide)
- [ ] 🔴 **Licensing check:** holding customer funds, wallets, lending, investment advice, crypto, or cross-border transfers may need a licence or a regulated partner (RBI PA/PPI rules in India, money transmitter licences in the US, EMI in the EU). → lawyer.
- [ ] 🟡 **KYC/AML** obligations if you onboard payees or hold funds.
- [ ] 🟡 **Financial advice disclaimers,** and no guaranteed-return claims.
- [ ] 🟡 **Data retention** for financial records follows the legal minimums (often 5–8 years), while other personal data is still minimized.

<a id="policies"></a>

## Policy pack: what the app must publish

Check which documents exist, whether they're linked where users need them, and whether **each one matches the data map and features from Step 2**. A generic template that doesn't match the app is a FAIL. Drafting is allowed only on request and only from the real data map; mark drafts "DRAFT – needs legal review" and add them to the "Needs a lawyer" list.

| Document | Needed when | Must include | Link it from |
|---|---|---|---|
| 🔴 **Privacy policy** | Always, if any personal data is collected | Each data type and its purpose, legal basis, processors and third parties (hosting, email, analytics, AI, fonts/CDNs), storage location and region, retention, user rights and how to use them, children's data (if any), a contact or grievance officer, an effective date | Footer, signup, every form that collects data |
| 🔴 **Terms of service** | Always | Who can use it, acceptable use, accounts, payments (if any), IP, termination, disclaimers, liability limit, governing law, contact | Footer, signup |
| 🔴 **Cookie notice and consent** | Non-essential cookies or trackers, with EU/UK users (and good practice everywhere) | Each cookie and tracker with its purpose and duration, plus a consent banner that blocks non-essential cookies until consent | Footer and the banner |
| 🔴 **Refund and cancellation policy** | The app charges money | How to cancel, refund rules, trial terms, processing times | Pricing, checkout, footer |
| 🔴 **Parental consent notice** | Minors (see the **kids-education** section) | What is collected about the child, why, retention, rights, how to withdraw consent | The consent flow |
| 🟡 **Acceptable use policy** | User-generated content, uploads or messaging | Prohibited content and behaviour, enforcement, how to report | Terms, report flow |
| 🟡 **Content and copyright takedown** | Users upload content | A contact for copyright and abuse reports, the process and timelines | Footer |
| 🟡 **AI disclosure** | AI features | What's AI-generated, its limits, which providers, whether data is used for training | Near the AI feature, privacy policy |
| 🟡 **Disclaimer** | Health, finance, legal, safety or fitness advice | "Not professional advice", plus when to see a professional | Near the advice |
| 🟡 **Accessibility statement** | Public, government or EU-market apps | Standard targeted (WCAG 2.2 AA), known gaps, contact | Footer |
| 🟡 **Data retention schedule** | Personal data is kept | How long each data type is kept and how it's deleted | Privacy policy, or internal |
| 🟡 **Subprocessor list** | B2B customers | Every vendor that processes customer data, with its location | Trust or legal page |
| 🟡 **Data processing agreement (DPA)** | B2B customers process personal data through you | Roles, security measures, subprocessors, breach notice, deletion | Available on request |
| 🟡 **`security.txt` and a vulnerability disclosure policy** | Public apps | A contact for security reports (`/.well-known/security.txt`) | Footer, or the well-known path |
| 🟡 **Incident response plan** | Personal data or paid users | Who does what, notification timelines (e.g. GDPR 72h, CERT-In 6h in India), contacts | Internal |
| 🟢 **Status page and SLA** | Paid or B2B | Uptime target, credits if any, how incidents are communicated | Footer, contracts |

### Checks
- [ ] 🔴 **Required documents exist:** each one is reachable without logging in and linked where users need it.
- [ ] 🔴 **Documents match the app today,** including recently added fields and features (compare against the data map).
- [ ] 🔴 **No overstated claims:** ("cannot be reversed", "fully anonymous", "bank-grade", "100% secure", "compliant" without proof).
- [ ] 🟡 **Dated and versioned:** each document has an effective date and a version history, and users are told about material changes.
- [ ] 🟡 **In the users' languages,** and the translations match.
- [ ] 🟡 **Contact details work** (the email inbox is monitored).

<a id="enterprise"></a>

## Enterprise and government readiness

Load this file at the Enterprise level: when selling to companies or government, or handling regulated data. This is what security reviews, procurement teams and auditors ask for. Many items are documents and processes, not code: mark them CAN'T TELL and ask the user.

### Identity and access
- [ ] 🔴 **SSO** (SAML or OIDC) for customer orgs, with **SCIM** or just-in-time provisioning and deprovisioning.
- [ ] 🔴 **Role-based access** with org-level admins. Customer admins can manage their own users.
- [ ] 🔴 **MFA required** for your own admin and production access (cloud, GitHub, DB, domain registrar, email).
- [ ] 🟡 **Access reviews** every quarter. Leavers are removed the same day. Shared accounts are avoided.
- [ ] 🟡 **Break-glass** emergency access is documented and logged.

### Audit and integrity
- [ ] 🔴 **Audit logs** for security-relevant events (logins, permission changes, exports, deletions, admin actions), exportable to the customer.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tamper evidence:** logs are append-only, or copied somewhere admins of the main system can't change them (a separate account, object lock, hash chaining). Be accurate about what "tamper-proof" means.
- [ ] 🟡 **Change management:** PR review required, protected branches, deploys traceable to commits, separation of duties for production changes.

### Data
- [ ] 🔴 **Data residency** known and matching what customers or regulations require (e.g. India-only hosting for some government work).
- [ ] 🔴 **Encryption** at rest and in transit, with key management documented.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tenant isolation** proven by tests: no cross-tenant reads in APIs, search, exports, caches or AI features.
- [ ] 🟡 **Customer data export and deletion** at contract end, with a timeline.
- [ ] 🟡 **Backups:** encrypted and off-site, with documented RPO and RTO targets (how much data you could lose, how long recovery takes), and **restore tested** with a date recorded.

### Security program
- [ ] 🟡 **Threat model** for the main flows and data.
- [ ] 🟡 **Third-party penetration test** within the last 12 months, with findings fixed. Government work may need a CERT-In-empanelled auditor or STQC.
- [ ] 🟡 **Vulnerability management:** dependency scanning in CI, an SLA for patching criticals, `security.txt`.
- [ ] 🟡 **SBOM** (software bill of materials) can be generated (e.g. `npx @cyclonedx/cyclonedx-npm`).
- [ ] 🟡 **Secrets management:** a vault or platform secrets, rotation schedule, no shared credentials.
- [ ] 🟡 **Incident response plan** with notification timelines, tested with a tabletop exercise.
- [ ] 🟢 **SOC 2 Type II / ISO 27001 readiness.** Policies exist (access, change, incident, vendor, BCP). A compliance tool (Vanta, Drata, Sprinto) helps when a certification is needed.

### Reliability and operations
- [ ] 🟡 **SLOs and an SLA**, uptime monitoring, a public status page, and incident communication.
- [ ] 🟡 **Disaster recovery and business continuity plan:** a cloud region failure, a lost account, a key person unavailable.
- [ ] 🟡 **Bus factor and ownership:** at least two people can access and operate production. The domain, cloud, code and DB are owned by the company, not a personal account. No production on a personal-name domain.
- [ ] 🟡 **Environments:** dev, staging and production are isolated, with no production data in lower environments (or it's masked).

### Procurement and compliance documents
- [ ] 🟡 **Security questionnaire answers** ready (CAIQ, SIG-lite, or the customer's own).
- [ ] 🟡 **Trust page:** security overview, subprocessors, DPA, status, contact.
- [ ] 🟡 **Accessibility conformance report** (VPAT or WCAG report). Government sites may need GIGW (India), Section 508 (US) or EN 301 549 (EU).
- [ ] 🟡 **Insurance** (cyber, professional liability) if contracts require it. → ask the user.

<a id="mobile"></a>

## Mobile apps (iOS and Android)

Load this file when the project contains a mobile app: native iOS or Android, React Native, Expo, Capacitor, Ionic, Flutter or a store-published PWA wrapper. App store rejections and mobile-only security problems are among the most common launch blockers. Check `Info.plist`, `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`, `AndroidManifest.xml`, `app.json`/`app.config.*`, `capacitor.config.*`, `build.gradle`, and the native project folders.

### App store requirements
- [ ] 🔴 **Account deletion inside the app** if the app lets people create accounts (Apple and Google both require it). The flow deletes server data too, not just the local session.
- [ ] 🔴 **Permission purpose strings for every permission the app can trigger:** `NSCameraUsageDescription`, `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription`, `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription`, `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription` and so on, in clear words. A missing string makes iOS **crash** the app when that feature is used (for example "Take Photo" in a file picker on iPad).
- [ ] 🔴 **Privacy labels match reality:** App Store privacy "nutrition labels" and the Google Play Data safety form list every data type collected, including by SDKs (analytics, crash reporting, ads, AI, auth).
- [ ] 🔴 **Apple privacy manifest** (`PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`) for the app, declaring required-reason APIs and tracking domains, and third-party SDKs that ship their own manifests are up to date.
- [ ] 🔴 **Digital goods use in-app purchase** where the store rules require it, and receipts or purchase tokens are **verified on the server**, never trusted from the device.
- [ ] 🔴 **Tracking permission:** if the app tracks users across other companies' apps or websites (ad SDKs, cross-app attribution), it asks with App Tracking Transparency on iOS before tracking.
- [ ] 🟡 **Sign in with Apple** is offered on iOS where Apple's rules require it (when other third-party social logins are offered).
- [ ] 🟡 **Target and minimum SDK versions** meet Google Play's current target API requirement and Apple's current SDK requirement.
- [ ] 🟡 **Store listing is honest:** screenshots, description, age rating and content questionnaire match the app (e.g. user chat or AI features change the rating). A support URL and privacy policy URL work.
- [ ] 🟡 **Review notes and a demo account** are prepared for app review if features need sign-in.

### Mobile security
- [ ] 🔴 **No secrets in the app bundle:** API secret keys, service-role keys and private tokens aren't in the JS bundle, `.env` files compiled into the app, `Info.plist`, `strings.xml` or `google-services.json` beyond the public config. Anything shipped in an app can be extracted.
- [ ] 🔴 **Tokens stored securely:** session and refresh tokens live in Keychain/Keystore (e.g. `expo-secure-store`, `@capacitor/preferences` with secure storage, `flutter_secure_storage`), not AsyncStorage, `localStorage` or plain files.
- [ ] 🔴 **HTTPS only:** no `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` / `usesCleartextTraffic="true"` in production builds, except narrowly for documented local development.
- [ ] 🟡 **Deep links and universal/app links are verified** (`apple-app-site-association`, `assetlinks.json`), and parameters from links are validated before use (no account actions straight from a link).
- [ ] 🟡 **WebViews are locked down:** no JavaScript bridges exposed to remote content, only trusted domains loaded, and file access disabled unless needed.
- [ ] 🟡 **Debug features are off in release builds:** no dev menus, debug logs with personal data, test endpoints or `debuggable=true`.
- [ ] 🟢 **Screenshots and app switcher** hide sensitive screens (payment, health, private messages) where appropriate.

### Reliability and quality
- [ ] 🔴 **Crash and error reporting** (Sentry, Crashlytics or similar) in release builds, with personal data scrubbed.
- [ ] 🟡 **Forced update path:** the app can tell users to update when an old version is no longer supported, and the API stays backwards-compatible with versions still in use.
- [ ] 🟡 **Offline and poor network:** the app shows clear states, doesn't lose drafts, and retries safely (no double payments).
- [ ] 🟡 **Permissions are requested in context** (when the feature is first used), with a friendly explanation and a way forward if refused.
- [ ] 🟡 **Tested on real devices:** small and large phones, tablets (iPad split view and rotation), the oldest supported OS version, and dark mode.
- [ ] 🟢 **App size and startup time** are reasonable, and large assets load on demand.
- [ ] 🟢 **Push notifications** ask permission in context, can be turned off in settings, and don't show sensitive content on the lock screen.

<a id="community"></a>

## Community, chat and marketplaces

Load this file when users can post content others see, message each other, join groups, follow each other, review things, or buy and sell from each other. These apps carry abuse, safety and legal duties that ordinary apps don't. Flag legal judgement calls for a lawyer.

### Safety tools
- [ ] 🔴 **Report button** on every piece of user content, profile and message, with a reason and an acknowledgement to the reporter.
- [ ] 🔴 **Block and mute:** users can block others, and blocking stops messages, mentions, follows and seeing each other's content.
- [ ] 🔴 **Moderation queue:** staff can review reports, remove content, warn, suspend and ban, with an audit log of every action and the reason.
- [ ] 🔴 **Automatic filtering** of obvious abuse: slurs, spam links, scams, and sexual or violent content in text and images (e.g. a moderation API or model), with stricter defaults where minors are present.
- [ ] 🔴 **Minors and adults:** if under-18s can use the app, check who can message them, whether adults can find or contact minors, and whether there's age-appropriate default privacy (see the **kids-education** section).
- [ ] 🟡 **Appeals:** users told why content was removed or an account suspended, with a way to appeal.
- [ ] 🟡 **New-account limits:** rate limits on posts, messages, invites and links for new or unverified accounts, to slow spam and raids.
- [ ] 🟡 **Images and files:** uploads are scanned or moderated before others see them, and EXIF/GPS metadata is stripped (see the **security** section).

### Chat and real-time
- [ ] 🔴 **WebSocket and real-time channels check authorization** on connect **and** on every subscribe or message: users can only join rooms, channels and conversations they belong to (Supabase Realtime, Firebase, Pusher, Socket.IO, Ably).
- [ ] 🔴 **Message rate limits and size limits** per user and per room.
- [ ] 🟡 **Message deletion and retention:** users can delete their messages, deleted messages are really removed, and the retention period is stated in the privacy policy.
- [ ] 🟡 **Links in messages** are shown safely (no auto-loading of untrusted previews from the server without SSRF protection).

### Legal duties (flag for a lawyer)
- [ ] 🔴 **Notice and takedown:** a way for anyone (not just users) to report illegal content or copyright infringement, with a contact address and response timeline. → lawyer.
- [ ] 🔴 **Child sexual abuse material:** a process to remove and report it to the authorities as the law requires (e.g. NCMEC in the US). Never just delete silently. → lawyer.
- [ ] 🟡 **Platform rules by region:** EU Digital Services Act (notice-and-action, statements of reasons, transparency), UK Online Safety Act (risk assessments, reporting, child safety), India IT Rules (grievance officer, takedown timelines). → lawyer.
- [ ] 🟡 **Community guidelines and terms** state what's not allowed and what happens, and they're linked at sign-up and in the report flow.

### Marketplaces and paid creators
- [ ] 🔴 **Seller or creator verification** before payouts (identity and bank details), with payout holds for new accounts and manual review of unusual amounts (see the **fintech** section).
- [ ] 🔴 **Money flows checked by a lawyer:** if users pay other users, or credits can be turned into money, check whether the business needs a payments licence or a regulated partner. → lawyer.
- [ ] 🟡 **Disputes and refunds** between buyers and sellers have a clear process and timeline.
- [ ] 🟡 **Fake reviews and ratings:** only real buyers or users can review, and incentivised reviews are labelled.
- [ ] 🟡 **Prohibited items and services** are listed and enforced (weapons, drugs, counterfeit goods, adult services where not allowed).
- [ ] 🟢 **Seller information** shown to buyers where e-commerce rules require it (e.g. EU, India).

<a id="cloud-infra"></a>

## Cloud infrastructure (AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, Kubernetes)

Load this file when the app runs on AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, DigitalOcean, Hetzner or similar, or uses Kubernetes, Terraform, Pulumi, CloudFormation or Docker Compose in production. Platform apps (Vercel, Netlify, Supabase, Firebase, Cloudflare) are covered in the **platforms** section. Read the IaC files and deployment configs. If the user has the cloud CLI logged in, run **read-only** commands only (`aws s3api get-bucket-policy-status`, `gcloud … describe`, `az … show`), never anything that changes resources.

### Storage
- [ ] 🔴 **No public buckets with private data:** S3 Block Public Access on (account and bucket), GCS uniform access without `allUsers`/`allAuthenticatedUsers`, Azure containers not set to public access. Public assets live in a separate, clearly public bucket.
- [ ] 🔴 **Cloud storage and disks encrypted** (buckets, databases, disks and snapshots) (default encryption on, customer-managed keys for sensitive data if required).
- [ ] 🟡 **Versioning and lifecycle rules** on important buckets, so deleted or overwritten files can be recovered and old data expires on schedule.
- [ ] 🟡 **Signed URLs** with short expiry for private files, never long-lived public links.

### Identity and access (IAM)
- [ ] 🔴 **No wildcard permissions** for app roles (`"Action": "*"`, `"Resource": "*"`, Owner/Editor roles for service accounts).
- [ ] 🔴 **No long-lived access keys in code or CI:** use instance roles, workload identity or OIDC for CI. Old keys are rotated or deleted.
- [ ] 🔴 **Root/owner account locked down:** MFA on, no access keys, used only for break-glass. Billing alerts set.
- [ ] 🟡 **Separate accounts or projects** for production and non-production.
- [ ] 🟡 **Cloud audit logs on** (CloudTrail, Cloud Audit Logs, Azure Activity Log) and kept for at least 90 days.

### Network
- [ ] 🔴 **Databases, caches and admin ports aren't open to the internet:** no `0.0.0.0/0` on 5432, 3306, 27017, 6379, 9200, 22 or RDP. Databases are in private subnets.
- [ ] 🔴 **TLS on every public endpoint** (load balancers, API gateways, CDNs), with HTTP redirected and old TLS versions disabled.
- [ ] 🟡 **SSH access** by key only (or through a bastion or session manager), no password login, no root login.
- [ ] 🟡 **WAF or rate limiting** at the edge for public APIs and login.

### Kubernetes and containers
- [ ] 🔴 **Secrets aren't in images or plain manifests:** use Kubernetes Secrets with encryption at rest, or an external secret manager. `docker history` shows no secrets.
- [ ] 🔴 **Containers don't run as root or privileged** unless required (`runAsNonRoot`, no `privileged: true`, read-only root filesystem where possible).
- [ ] 🟡 **Resource requests and limits** set, so one service can't starve others.
- [ ] 🟡 **Images from trusted registries,** pinned by digest or version (not `latest`), and scanned for vulnerabilities.
- [ ] 🟡 **The Kubernetes API and dashboards aren't public,** and RBAC follows least privilege.

### Infrastructure as Code
- [ ] 🟡 **IaC scanned** for misconfigurations (e.g. `tfsec`, `checkov`, `trivy config`), and state files (Terraform state) stored privately and encrypted, since they contain secrets.
- [ ] 🟡 **Changes go through review and CI,** not manual console edits (drift is detected).
- [ ] 🟡 **Backups and snapshots are automatic** for databases and volumes, and a restore has been tested (see the **devops** section).
- [ ] 🟢 **Cost controls:** budgets and alerts, auto-scaling limits, and no forgotten resources (unused instances, load balancers, IPs, snapshots).

<a id="desktop-extensions"></a>

## Desktop apps and browser extensions

Load this file when the project is an Electron, Tauri or other desktop app, or a browser extension (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari). These run with more power on the user's computer than a website does, so mistakes are more dangerous.

### Electron and Tauri
- [ ] 🔴 **Electron isolation on:** `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, `sandbox: true` for every window. Renderer code can't call Node APIs directly.
- [ ] 🔴 **Safe preload bridge:** `contextBridge` exposes only specific, validated functions, never `ipcRenderer` itself or generic "run this command" or "read this file" functions.
- [ ] 🔴 **No remote content with privileges:** the app doesn't load remote websites in privileged windows. External links open in the system browser (`shell.openExternal` with an allow-list of protocols), and navigation and new windows are restricted.
- [ ] 🔴 **Code signing and notarization:** builds are signed (Apple Developer ID + notarization on macOS, Authenticode on Windows), so users aren't trained to bypass security warnings.
- [ ] 🔴 **Secure auto-update:** updates come over HTTPS from a trusted source and are signature-checked (electron-updater with signing, Tauri updater with public key).
- [ ] 🟡 **Tauri allow-lists:** only the APIs, file paths and shell commands the app needs are enabled in the Tauri config or capabilities.
- [ ] 🟡 **Content Security Policy** set for app windows, and no `eval` or remote scripts.
- [ ] 🟡 **Local data protection:** tokens in the OS keychain (`keytar`, `safeStorage`, Tauri stronghold or keyring), not plain files. Local databases with sensitive data are encrypted.
- [ ] 🟡 **Electron is kept up to date** (old Chromium versions carry known vulnerabilities).
- [ ] 🟢 **Crash reporting and a way to collect logs** with the user's consent.

### Browser extensions
- [ ] 🔴 **Minimal permissions:** only the `permissions` and `host_permissions` the extension really needs. No `<all_urls>` unless essential (prefer `activeTab` and optional permissions requested in context).
- [ ] 🔴 **Manifest V3** for Chrome and Edge, with no remotely hosted code (all scripts packaged), as store policies require.
- [ ] 🔴 **Content scripts don't trust the page:** messages from web pages (`window.postMessage`) are validated, and page data is never passed to `eval`, `innerHTML` or privileged APIs without checking the sender and content.
- [ ] 🔴 **Data handling matches the store disclosure:** the Chrome Web Store privacy practices form, Firefox data collection disclosure and the privacy policy describe exactly what's collected (browsing history, page content), and limited-use rules are followed.
- [ ] 🟡 **Secrets aren't in the extension package:** API keys used by the extension are public-safe or go through your server.
- [ ] 🟡 **External messaging restricted:** `externally_connectable` lists only your own domains, if used.
- [ ] 🟡 **Store listing is accurate:** single, clear purpose, honest screenshots and description, a support contact.
- [ ] 🟢 **Update and rollback plan:** store review can delay fixes, so risky features are behind server-side flags.

<a id="i18n"></a>

## Internationalisation and localisation

Load this file when the app supports more than one language or region, sells to several countries, or targets users whose language isn't English. Translation gaps and wrong formats look broken to users, and in some places (e.g. Quebec) missing language versions are a legal problem (see the **lawsuits** section).

### Language
- [ ] 🔴 **No missing translations:** every visible string in each supported language, including errors, emails, notifications, buttons, empty states, legal pages and app store listings. No raw keys like `checkout.button.pay` or mixed languages on one screen.
- [ ] 🔴 **Legal and consent text in each language users get,** and the translations say the same thing as the original (privacy policy, terms, consent banner, parental consent).
- [ ] 🟡 **Strings aren't hard-coded** in components. They come from translation files, so new languages can be added.
- [ ] 🟡 **Plurals and grammar handled properly** (ICU message format or the library's plural rules), not `count + " items"`.
- [ ] 🟡 **Language selection:** detected sensibly (browser or device language), changeable by the user, remembered, and the `lang` attribute is set on pages for screen readers and SEO.
- [ ] 🟡 **Machine-translated text reviewed** by a fluent speaker for key flows (sign-up, payment, legal).
- [ ] 🟢 **SEO for languages:** separate URLs per language and `hreflang` tags (see the **launch-ux** section).

### Layout and text
- [ ] 🔴 **Scripts render correctly:** non-Latin scripts (Hindi, Tamil, Arabic, Chinese and others) display with suitable fonts, with no boxes (tofu) or broken characters in UI, PDFs, emails and exports.
- [ ] 🟡 **Right-to-left languages** (Arabic, Hebrew, Urdu) mirror the layout correctly if supported (`dir="rtl"`, logical CSS properties).
- [ ] 🟡 **Longer text fits:** layouts handle translations that are 30–50% longer than English (German, Tamil) without overflow or cut-off buttons.
- [ ] 🟡 **Search, sorting and validation** work for non-English text (accents, case, local sorting order, names that don't fit "first name / last name").

### Formats and regional rules
- [ ] 🔴 **Prices and currencies are right:** the correct currency and symbol, local number format (e.g. 1,00,000 in India and 100.000,00 in Germany), and taxes shown the way each region expects (prices with or without VAT/GST).
- [ ] 🟡 **Dates, times and time zones** are shown in the user's format and time zone (see the **functional-qa** section).
- [ ] 🟡 **Phone numbers and addresses** accept international formats (E.164 for phones), with no US-only fields like "State" or "ZIP" made mandatory for everyone.
- [ ] 🟡 **Units** (metric or imperial, sizes) suit the region if the app shows them.
- [ ] 🟡 **Payment methods** fit each region (UPI in India, iDEAL in the Netherlands, local cards), if the app sells there.
- [ ] 🟢 **Regional content and features** that are restricted or different per country (age limits, banned products, data residency) are handled.

